  "result": "**Root cause:** `parseAmount` in `src/money.js` rejected prices with thousands separators, such as `\"2,450.50\"`. Its regex only allowed digits, so `match()` returned `null` and `m[1]` threw `TypeError: Cannot read properties of null (reading '1')`. `checkout` caught that and returned a 500.\n\nThe log shows 37 such 500s. Every failing payload had one of three prices: `1,299.00` (15 times), `2,450.50` (12) and `1,005.99` (10). No other price in the log fails, so the errors only look intermittent because most carts have no price of 1,000 or more.\n\n**Fix (`src/money.js`):**\n- `parseAmount` now accepts properly grouped thousands (`1,299.00`, `1,000,000`) and strips the commas.\n- Malformed input (`abc`, `12,34`, `-5`, empty) now throws `Error('Invalid amount: …')`, as the function's comment already promised, instead of a `TypeError`. `checkout` still returns a 500 for those.\n\n**Tests (`test/money.test.js`):** I added three tests:\n- Thousands-separator parsing, using the three prices from the log.\n- Rejection of malformed amounts.\n- A `checkout` test with a comma price.\n\n`npm test` passes with 7 of 7. I didn't run the new tests against the old code, but the first and third use the exact inputs that crashed it.\n\nI made no commit.",
